E=[
(1,11,1891,"Cosmos","Lescarbault's new star","Dr. Lescarbault announces he saw a new star on the night of Jan. 11. At the next meeting of the French Academy, Flammarion rises and says Lescarbault had 'discovered' Saturn. Fort uses it to show how astronomers settle old scores.","NL","New Lands","upon the night of Jan. 11, 1891, he had seen a new star"),
(2,6,1913,"Sky","A searchlight over the Dulais Valley","Police in the Dulais Valley, Wales report a searchlight-like beam and the object that flashed it, and a police sergeant describes a luminous thing that hung in the sky and then moved away. The Cardiff Evening Express printed it in the 1913 airship scare.","NL","New Lands, citing Cardiff Evening Express, Feb. 6, 1913","by the police of Dulais Valley"),
(2,21,1885,"Cosmos","Reddish smoke in the crater Cassini","Lorenzo Kropp, an astronomer at Paysandu, Uruguay, sees a reddish smoke or mist in the lunar crater Cassini. L'Astronomie published his account.","NL","New Lands, citing L'Astronomie, 1885-227","upon Feb. 21, 1885, he had seen, in Cassini"),
(2,26,1913,"Sky","A crowd watches a light over Hull","Crowds in Hull watch lights in the sky. The Hull Daily Mail says the light stayed stationary for almost half an hour and then shot away northward. Fort places it in the airship wave of February 1913.","NL","New Lands, citing Hull Daily Mail, Feb. 26, 1913","a crowd had watched a light high in the air"),
(2,28,1913,"Sky","An airship with a searchlight, from six places","The Times prints reports of 'the clear outlines of an airship, which was carrying a dazzling searchlight' from Portland, St. Alban's Head, the Orkneys and other places. The same column notes a fire-balloon found in Yorkshire.","NL","New Lands, citing London Times, Feb. 28, 1913","the clear outlines of an airship, which was carrying a dazzling searchlight"),
(3,2,1899,"Sky","A day-long light over El Paso","A luminous object in the sky from 10 a.m. until 4 p.m. is reported from El Paso, Texas. The Observatory mentioned it as supposed to have been Venus.","NL","New Lands, citing Observatory, 22-247","Upon March 2, 1899, a luminous object in the sky, from 10 A. M., until 4 P. M., was reported from El Paso, Texas"),
(3,10,1895,"Sky","Lightning from a ship during a lunar eclipse","During an eclipse of the Moon, with no cloud or mist, electric flashes like lightning are reported from a ship on the Atlantic. The English Mechanic reported it.","NL","New Lands, citing English Mechanic, 61-100","An eclipse of the moon, March 10-11, 1895"),
(4,12,1864,"Falls","A stone falls at Nerft","A stone falls at Nerft, Courland, one of four Russian stone falls of which Fort examined specimens. He found them very much alike and not of uncommon meteoritic material.","NL","New Lands, citing Comptes Rendus, 85-681","April 12, 1864, at Nerft, Courland"),
(5,5,1832,"Cosmos","Fisher's Mercury at Lisbon","Fisher at Lisbon reports a body crossing the disc of the Sun. According to the Nautical Almanac, it was Mercury. Fort uses such sightings to show how astronomers 'discover' Saturn and do not know Mercury.","NL","New Lands","Fisher Lisbon May 5, 1832"),
(5,9,1879,"Mysteries","The Astronomical Society turns on Sadler","At a meeting of the Society, an attack led by Chambers demands that Sadler resign for having slandered a great astronomer. Fort uses it as a case of the Society punishing a critic.","NL","New Lands","At the meeting of the Society, May 9, 1879"),
(5,24,1868,"Falls","A woman cut by a stone in Birmingham","During the stone falls of that spring, a woman walking down a lane is cut on the head by a stone. The writer blamed boys but does not say anyone saw one throw it. The Birmingham Daily Post printed the letter.","NL","New Lands, citing Birmingham Daily Post, June 4, 1868","his wife, while walking down a lane, upon May 24th, had been cut on the head by a stone"),
(5,26,1828,"Cosmos","Things crossed the Sun","Webb lists unknown bodies crossing the Sun on May 26, 1828 and on July 31, 1826. Fort adds them to his run of observations like Lescarbault's.","NL","New Lands, citing Comptes Rendus, 83-623 and Webb","Things that crossed the sun, July 31, 1826, and May 26, 1828"),
(6,1,1867,"Mysteries","A light in Woburn Square","The Lancet reports that every night for a week a light has appeared on the grass of a small railed park in Woburn Square, London, with crowds gathering and police called out.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Lancet, June 1, 1867","a light had appeared in Woburn Square, London"),
(6,2,1919,"Mysteries","Capt. James is lost in the Berkshires","The New York Times reports that Capt. Mansell R. James is lost somewhere in the Berkshire Hills on a flight from Boston to Atlantic City. Fort says the circumstances of the disappearance call for special attention.","NL","New Lands, citing New York Times, June 2, 1919","Capt. Mansell R. James was lost somewhere in the Berkshire Hills"),
(6,4,1919,"Mysteries","Army planes search for Capt. James","Army planes arrive and search the Berkshires systematically for Capt. Mansell James, with rewards offered. Search parties had already 'scoured' the hills without finding a trace.","NL","New Lands, on the disappearance of Capt. James","Upon June 4th, army planes arrived and searched systematically"),
(6,5,1867,"Sky","Mr. Bird: not seeds, but meteors","In the Birmingham Post, Mr. Bird replies to a critic that the objects he saw were not seeds: 'the appearance was certainly that of meteors.' Fort admires him for simply describing what was seen.","NL","New Lands, citing Birmingham Daily Post, June 5, 1867","Post, June 5-Mr. Bird says that the objects were not seeds"),
(6,22,1884,"Mysteries","Darkness and soot at Fletching","At Fletching, Sussex, intense darkness falls, and rain then brings down flakes of soot in such abundance that Fort lists it among the black rains and black days of 1884. Nature reported it.","NL","New Lands, citing Nature, 30-216","upon June 22, 1884, at Fletching, Sussex"),
(7,7,1857,"Haunts","A man followed by a pond crosses the Moon","Two people at Chambon see forms cross the Moon: 'something like a human being followed by a pond'. Fort cites Cosmos in his chapter on things crossing the Moon and Sun.","NL","New Lands, citing Cosmos, n.s., 11-200","night of July 7, 1857, two persons of Chambon had seen forms crossing the moon"),
(8,3,1886,"Cosmos","Bodies streaming from the Sun","M. Poey cites a similar instance to the 1836 Havana eclipse, when Prof. Auber saw luminous bodies move out from the Sun in different directions. M. Lotard thought they were birds.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing L'Astronomie, 1886-391","M. Poey cites a similar instance, of Aug. 3, 1886"),
(8,4,1857,"Falls","A 25-pound block of pure ice at Cricklewood","The Times reports a block of 'pure' ice weighing 25 pounds found in Mr. Warner's meadow at Cricklewood after the previous day's storm.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing London Times, Aug. 4, 1857","weighing 25 pounds, had been found in the meadow of Mr. Warner, of Cricklewood"),
(12,23,1909,"Sky","A mysterious airship at Worcester","The New York Tribune reports that a 'mysterious airship' appeared over Worcester, Massachusetts, 'sweeping the heavens with a searchlight of tremendous power', coming from the south.","NL","New Lands, citing New York Tribune, Dec. 23, 1909","had appeared over the town of Worcester, Mass."),
]
